// std/function.bpa — the theory of MAPPINGS between sets, per Chapter 1 of
// Judson's *Abstract Algebra* (vendor/aata/src/sets.xml, §1.2.2 "Cartesian
// Products and Mappings"): functions as objects with application, composition,
// an identity map, and the injective / surjective / invertible properties.
//
// A reusable abstract theory (like std/group, std/set), imported by the AATA
// literate translation aata/functions.md. Every function here maps a fixed
// element domain `Universe` to itself (f : Universe -> Universe); that single
// sort is enough for composition-associativity and the invertible/bijective
// results, and keeps the theory small. Functions are opaque `Fn` objects
// characterized by what `apply` does to them, so two functions are equal
// exactly when they agree pointwise (funcExtensionality) — the mapping analogue
// of set extensionality.
//
// bpa has no `<->` connective, so each defining biconditional (injective,
// surjective, invertible) is written as the `and` of its two implications.

sort Universe
sort Fn
const identityFn: Fn
func apply(f: Fn, x: Universe): Universe
func compose(g: Fn, f: Fn): Fn
pred injective(f: Fn)
pred surjective(f: Fn)
pred invertible(f: Fn)

// (g ∘ f)(x) = g(f(x)) — the defining law of composition.
axiom composeApply: forall g, f: Fn; forall x: Universe;
  apply(compose(g, f), x) = apply(g, apply(f, x))

// id(x) = x — the identity map does nothing.
axiom identityApply: forall x: Universe; apply(identityFn, x) = x

// function extensionality: maps agreeing at every point are equal.
axiom funcExtensionality: forall f, g: Fn;
  (forall x: Universe; apply(f, x) = apply(g, x)) -> f = g

// injective (one-to-one): f(a) = f(b) implies a = b (and conversely, equal
// inputs give equal outputs — the trivial direction, included so the predicate
// unfolds to a full biconditional).
axiom injectiveDef: forall f: Fn;
  (injective(f) -> (forall a, b: Universe; apply(f, a) = apply(f, b) -> a = b))
  and ((forall a, b: Universe; apply(f, a) = apply(f, b) -> a = b) -> injective(f))

// surjective (onto): every codomain element y is hit by some x.
axiom surjectiveDef: forall f: Fn;
  (surjective(f) -> (forall y: Universe; exists x: Universe; apply(f, x) = y))
  and ((forall y: Universe; exists x: Universe; apply(f, x) = y) -> surjective(f))

// invertible: there is a two-sided inverse g (g∘f = id and f∘g = id).
axiom invertibleDef: forall f: Fn;
  (invertible(f) ->
  (exists g: Fn; compose(g, f) = identityFn and compose(f, g) = identityFn))
  and ((exists g: Fn; compose(g, f) = identityFn and compose(f, g) = identityFn)
  -> invertible(f))
